WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.098
Start and controls
Take a seat and press Ready. The host starts when everyone is ready. Your cards or dice are at the bottom.

00:00:13.098 --> 00:00:19.397
Follow the turn prompt. Wait for animations to finish; an expired turn is played automatically. Use Return to room after settlement.

00:00:19.397 --> 00:00:31.400
Land Battle Chess: deploy, then fight
We keep red’s perspective. Each side has 25 pieces; only our own identities are visible. The server referees the hidden enemy ranks.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:43.400
Goal: capture the enemy flag
Take the opposing flag or leave the opponent no legal move to win. Both sides must submit legal deployments before alternating moves.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:56.600
Tap two stations to swap pieces
Choose a preset or Auto, or tap two friendly pieces to swap them. This example uses a legal automatically generated deployment.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:07.400
Flags belong in headquarters, mines in the last two rows, and bombs off the front row. Camps start empty. Invalid swaps explain the restriction.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:23.399
Confirm deployment to lock it in
Both players confirm independently and cannot edit afterwards. Enemy readiness does not reveal ranks. Play starts only when both are ready.

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:41.399
Select your piece and a legal target
Select a friendly piece to see destinations, then choose one. Mouse submits immediately; touch previews before Confirm or a second tap.

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:51.299
Faster play: roads, rails and camps
Roads move one station; railways allow longer straight moves without jumping pieces. Speed through both sides’ initial manoeuvres.

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:59.399
Pieces in camps cannot be attacked. Mines, flags and any piece inside headquarters cannot move.

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:15.398
The first battle reveals only its outcome
Red is about to attack a blue piece on the front. The referee reports attacker win, loss or mutual removal, without naming the enemy rank.

00:02:15.398 --> 00:02:27.498
Faster battles: ranks and special pieces
Follow the referee’s outcomes and removed pieces. Enemy appearances do not establish identities; public movements and battle results are your clues.

00:02:27.498 --> 00:02:37.399
Higher rank wins; equal ranks remove both. Bombs remove both fighters. Engineers clear mines; other ranks die attacking them. Any piece can take a flag.

00:02:37.399 --> 00:02:47.299
Engineers can turn along railways
Watch our engineer manoeuvre at higher speed. Choose its legal final station; the server finds the route, and the move list records stations passed.

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:55.398
Only engineers may turn along rails. No railway path can pass through another piece.

00:02:55.398 --> 00:03:15.397
Faster attacks, with enemy ranks still hidden
Every upcoming move retains red’s filtered view. An enemy piece that moved cannot be a mine or flag, but its exact rank remains hidden.

00:03:15.397 --> 00:03:24.197
Blue’s fallen marshal reveals its flag
The latest mutual removal killed blue’s marshal, so blue’s flag location is now public. Other unrevealed ranks stay covered.

00:03:24.197 --> 00:03:31.397
A marshal’s death reveals that side’s flag. Ordinary battles do not turn every enemy piece face up.

00:03:31.397 --> 00:03:41.297
Fast-forward through the final manoeuvres
Both flags have now been revealed. Speed through the remaining moves and battles as red approaches blue’s headquarters.

00:03:41.297 --> 00:03:49.397
A run of 60 plies without battle draws. A draw offer also requires the opponent’s agreement.

00:03:49.397 --> 00:04:01.397
The final flag capture
Red is beside blue’s revealed flag. It is red’s turn to attack the headquarters and take that publicly known target.

00:04:01.397 --> 00:04:16.400
Result: red captures the flag
After deployment and 137 moves, red takes the flag. Only now are all surviving ranks revealed. Review the reason and move count, then return to the room.
